of stress people endured.


01:26:21.391 --> 01:26:23.660
All to keep
the economic machine moving.


01:26:24.595 --> 01:26:26.998
One thing that's
really interesting in all this,


01:26:27.030 --> 01:26:28.765
is that the economic
system back then


01:26:28.798 --> 01:26:31.968
embraced a fantastic paradox.


01:26:32.002 --> 01:26:33.604
So here you had a model


01:26:33.637 --> 01:26:35.406
that defended itself
by the assumption


01:26:35.439 --> 01:26:38.575
of universal scarcity.
Forcing competition,


01:26:38.609 --> 01:26:40.845
oppression, exploitation,


01:26:40.878 --> 01:26:43.781
poverty and so on,
while at the same time


01:26:43.814 --> 01:26:47.851
the entire machine needed
infinite consumption to work.


01:26:47.885 --> 01:26:49.820
Think about that for a moment.


01:26:49.852 --> 01:26:53.423
How do you justify these harsh
outcomes of assumed scarcity


01:26:53.457 --> 01:26:56.526
when the system itself
disregards the idea entirely


01:26:56.560 --> 01:26:59.297
when it comes to the very
mechanics that make it work?


01:26:59.329 --> 01:27:02.365
And the bottom line is
this wasn't an economy at all


01:27:02.399 --> 01:27:05.569
